t but their prebiotic Orn sourcing is weakly supported and unbalanced as written. Metabolic and high-T modules accurately reflect their current experimental status\u2014reasonable for inclusion, but low in Arg-specific confidence. The nitrate/formaldehyde and FTT pathways are properly included as historical but ranked very low due to modern analytical standards.\\n\\nKey improvements: Tighten atom/charge balancing for all steps, especially intermediate transformations and Orn formation. Where literature only supports a precursor (e.g., \u03b1-aminonitrile), represent this as an endpoint rather than a full Arg yield. Explicitly document all speculative nodes and ensure all explicit molecule formulas are deterministically interpretable. With these amendments, the network will rise to high-certainty approval. As written, it is comprehensive and chemically informed, but cannot be approved until mass/charge conservation and nomenclature issues are resolved.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.95, "rxn_002": 0.95, "rxn_003": 0.2, "rxn_004": 0.8, "rxn_005": 0.75, "rxn_006": 0.7, "rxn_007": 0.75, "rxn_008": 0.8, "rxn_009": 0.2}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.7, "pathway_coherence": 0.9, "environmental_progression": 0.9, "target_achievement": 0.8, "overall_confidence": 0.78, "feedback": "This pathway (Patel's cyanosulfidic) mirrors the experimentally supported progression to arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile, which is the firm product by literature. However, mass/charge conservation issues exist for several steps (notably rxn_003: incomplete balancing of redox/reduction; rxn_009: missing small molecules in hydrolysis), and several complex intermediates have unparsable formulas (rxn_004-rxn_008), creating ambiguity in atom tracking. Stepwise chemistry and environmental assignments are plausible given the known reactions, catalysts, and constraints. Final hydrolytic step (rxn_009) is plausible in concept but unbalance