m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Arginine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This network rigorously integrates ALL experimentally supported and plausible prebiotic modules for arginine, specifically: (1) The UV-driven cyanosulfidic protometabolic pathway of Patel et al. (2015), through stepwise C3\u2192C5 aminonitrile/aldehyde, with explicit H, N, and S atom flow, all intermediates, and properly detailed small-molecule accounting; (2) Late-stage guanidination of ornithine (Longo et al. 2020), with ornithine supply via both volcanic discharge (Johnson et al. 2008) and speculative, mass-balanced protometabolic transformation from glutamate; (3) A carefully detailed Fischer\u2013Tropsch\u2013type (FTT) direct synthesis (Yoshino et al. 1971), included as a historical low-confidence network with full balancing and realistic environmental agents; (4) The historical nitrate/formaldehyde photochemical route (Dhar, Mukherjee, Bahadur) is included as very low-confidence with explicit caveats. Each pathway is atom/charge balanced for all C/N/H/S main-chain transformations; intermediates are defined precisely, including \u03b1-aminonitrile, ornithine, cyanamide, and all C3\u2013C5 skeleton carriers. Networks are cross-linked only where credible supported chemistry allows. Pathway diversity is achieved by explicit environmental succession when justified (e.g., volcanic Orn formation, surface guanidination), and by including both early and late guanidination events (free Orn, Orn-peptide, and Glu-derived Orn). All agents are mapped to the earliest plausible minerals or chemical agents in each context, per cited literature.\n\nMOLECULES (30 nodes):\n  - mol_001: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: C2H2 (acetylene) [starting]\n  - mol_003: Cu+ (Cu(I) ion) [intermediate]\n  - mol_004: C3H3N (acrylonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_005: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_006: C3H6N2 (\u03b2-aminopropionitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_007: H2S (hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_008: C3H7NO (\u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_009: CH2N2 (cyanamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C4H10N4O (guanidinated aminopropionaldehyde) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C5H11N3O (guanidinated C5 cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: H2S (hydrogen sulfide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C5H11N3OS (guanidinated C5 thioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: NH3 (ammonia) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C6H14N4 (arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_017: H2O (water) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C6H14N4O2 (arginine) [target]\n  - mol_019: C5H12N2O2 (ornithine) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_020: CO (carbon monoxide) [starting]\n  - mol_021: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_022: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_023: Ni/Al2O3 (Ni on alumina) [intermediate]\n  - mol_024: C7H15N3O2 (ornithine peptide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_025: C7H15N3O2 (arginine peptide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_026: C3H6N2 (cyanamide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_027: C5H6N2O4 (\u03b1-ketoglutarate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_028: C5H9NO4 (glutamate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_029: NO3- (nitrate ion) [starting]\n  - mol_030: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n\nREACTIONS (18 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_0